{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**A Midsummer’s Journey to the Land of the Rising Sun**\n\n**Act I: Scene the First – Arrival in the Imperial City of Tokyo**\n\nHark! Thou dost set foot in Tokyo’s fair realm where neon lights do dance like sprites upon the eve. First to the ancient quarters of Asakusa do thou repair where the Senso-ji Temple stands in solemn grace. Let thy soul be cleansed at its sacred gate and partake of the finest confections at Nakamise-dori’s bustling lane.\n\nThen hie thee to the modern marvels of Shibuya where the famed crossing doth pulse with life like a great heartbeat of the world. Ascend to the Shibuya Sky for a vista most wondrous and behold the city spread before thee like a tapestry woven by the gods themselves.\n\n**Act I: Scene the Second – The Gardens of Tranquility and the Arts’ Embrace**\n\nOn the morrow journey to the Imperial Palace’s gardens where koi do glide through waters clear and peacocks strut with regal pride. Let thy spirit wander through the East Gardens’ quiet paths and meditate upon the fleeting nature of all earthly things.\n\nIn the eve repair to the Kabuki-za Theatre where players don masks of painted hue and voices rise in song and declamation. Let thy heart be stirred by tales of ancient feuds and lovers’ woes as they unfold upon the stage.\n\n**Act II: Scene the First – The Sacred Peaks of Mount Fuji**\n\nWith dawn’s first light set forth upon the road to Hakone where the great Mount Fuji doth rise like a sleeping titan clad in white. Take ship upon Lake Ashi’s waters and let the vistas of misty peaks and distant shores fill thy eyes with awe.\n\nAt Hakone’s open-air museum wander through sculptures that dance with light and shadow and bathe in waters hot and steaming that purge the flesh of toil and care.\n\n**Act III: Scene the First – The Ancient Capital of Kyoto**\n\nBy swift train do thou glide to Kyoto’s hallowed ground where temples old as time do stand in quiet majesty. First visit the Kinkaku-ji where the Golden Pavilion gleams like a jewel upon the earth and reflects its splendor in the still waters below.\n\nThen to the Arashiyama’s bamboo groves repair where towering stalks do whisper secrets to the wind. Walk the ancient Togetsukyo Bridge and let thy soul be soothed by nature’s gentle song.\n\nIn the eve don thy finest robes and venture to Gion’s lanes where geisha glide like specters of another age. Let thy senses be tantalized by the scent of incense and the taste of kaiseki’s finest dishes.\n\n**Act IV: Scene the First – The Path of the Pilgrim**\n\nUpon the morrow make pilgrimage to Nara where sacred deer do bow before thee and the great Todai-ji Temple stands in silent grandeur. Behold the colossal Buddha who watches over all with eyes serene and gaze upon the pagoda that pierces the heavens.\n\nThen to Osaka do thou wend thy way where the smell of takoyaki doth fill the air and the people’s mirth is loud and merry. Scale the Osaka Castle’s towering walls and let thy eyes feast upon the city’s sprawling embrace.\n\n**Epilogue: Scene the Last – Farewell to the Land of the Rising Sun**\n\nAs thy journey’s end doth near and the sun sinks low upon the horizon return to Tokyo’s embrace. In Shibuya’s wards do thou lastly sup at a sushi counter where the chef’s knife doth sing and the fish melt upon thy tongue like ambrosia of the gods.\n\nThen with a heart full and a soul enriched by wonders seen do thou depart from this fair land with memories that shall endure as long as stars do shine and rivers flow. Farewell sweet Japan and may fortune’s favor ever light thy path.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -1.763585090637207}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.0858144536614418}, {"token": " M", "logprob": -1.4411967992782593}, {"token": "ids", "logprob": -0.012354512698948383}, {"token": "ummer", "logprob": -0.0001821352052502334}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.054371